[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Technical Tutorial: Relational Model and ACID Properties in Database Design"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"This tutorial explores the foundational principles of the Relational Model and the ACID properties that guarantee reliable transaction processing in modern Database Management Systems (DBMS)."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"1. The Relational Model Overview"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"Introduced by Edgar F. Codd in 1970, the relational model represents data as a collection of relations, or tables. Each table consists of rows (tuples) and columns (attributes). This model abstracts the physical storage details, providing a logical, mathematically grounded structure for querying and updating data."},{"order":5,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Relation (Table): A set of tuples sharing the same attributes."},{"order":6,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Tuple (Row): A single entity or record within a relation."},{"order":7,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Attribute (Column): A specific property or data field of a relation."},{"order":8,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Domain: The set of allowable values for a given attribute."},{"order":9,"type":"subheading","text":"2. Database Transactions and ACID Properties"},{"order":10,"type":"paragraph","text":"A transaction is a logical unit of work comprising one or more database operations. To ensure data integrity, especially in concurrent environments and system failures, relational databases enforce the ACID properties."},{"order":11,"type":"subheading","text":"Atomicity"},{"order":12,"type":"paragraph","text":"Atomicity guarantees that all operations within a transaction complete successfully. If a single operation fails, the entire transaction is aborted (rolled back), and the database remains unchanged."},{"order":13,"type":"subheading","text":"Consistency"},{"order":14,"type":"paragraph","text":"Consistency ensures that a transaction brings the database from one valid state to another, maintaining all predefined rules, such as foreign keys, constraints, and triggers."},{"order":15,"type":"subheading","text":"Isolation"},{"order":16,"type":"paragraph","text":"Isolation determines how and when changes made by one transaction become visible to other concurrent transactions. It prevents issues such as dirty reads, non-repeatable reads, and phantom reads."},{"order":17,"type":"subheading","text":"Durability"},{"order":18,"type":"paragraph","text":"Durability guarantees that once a transaction has been committed, its results will persist permanently, even in the event of a system crash, power outage, or hardware failure."},{"order":19,"type":"subheading","text":"3. Practical Example: SQL Transaction"},{"order":20,"type":"paragraph","text":"The following SQL code demonstrates a transaction executing a bank transfer, utilizing atomicity, consistency, isolation, and durability."},{"order":21,"type":"code","text":"BEGIN TRANSACTION;

UPDATE Accounts 
SET balance = balance - 100.00 
WHERE account_id = 101;

UPDATE Accounts 
SET balance = balance + 100.00 
WHERE account_id = 102;

-- Check for errors or constraints
COMMIT TRANSACTION;"},{"order":22,"type":"subheading","text":"Conclusion"},{"order":23,"type":"paragraph","text":"Understanding the relational model and ACID properties is critical for designing robust, scalable, and error-free database systems. These paradigms remain the cornerstone of enterprise data management."}]
